A||N||4||TRL46202||88GT - Help!!!||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||11-22-2000||09:05 AM||||This car has a fast idle,cold or hot (2000). It's an automatic. I have replaced the IAC and egr (map sensor) runs great but idles toooo fast. Can anyone help????<P>Thanks, Tom||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||11-22-2000||09:15 AM||||If you replace the IAC the idle will be high. The reason is the ECM has to reset it. To have the ECM reset the IAC you need to drive the vehicle over 40mph for about 30 seconds. Just find a road that is 40 -45 mph speed limit and drive down it. When you stop the idle should return to normal.<BR>If your idle was high before and you replaced the above you may have a vacuum leak. Possibly the EGR tube or cracked vacuum line.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRL46202||11-22-2000||09:18 AM||||Thanks, did that and still nothing. Anything else???<P>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000003||lawrence||11-22-2000||01:59 PM||||My vote is a cracked EGR tube, as Oreif pointed out.  And that was my intent.<P>You can check the tube with a stethoscope.  Use a small diameter rubber hose, about 3 feet long.  Put one end of the hose in your ear (not too deep, of course) and run the other end along the EGR tube and you can hear the leak sucking air in.<P>If you find this, you have two choices.  You may be able to fix it by having it brazed, as I did about three years ago.  Or, you can  replace it with a new or used EGR tube.  I've also heard that you can modify a tube from a non-Fiero 2.8, but I don't have those details.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A>  - WebElectric Magazine<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-22-2000||02:02 PM||||Usually, idling <B>that</B> high is caused by vacuum leak(s).  Prime suspects: The EGR tube, PCV hoses (FWIW, once my PCV hose under the plenum fell off), and then the rest of the vacuum lines.  Visually inspect all of the vinyl lines under the plenum and their fittings.  If you're lucky, you may find a disconnected or cracked line - easy to fix  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>Use a piece of hose as a stethoscope (one end to your ear; snoop around with the other end), and listen for vacuum leaks while the engine idles.  That may help you locate a leak.   <P>Did you install the new EGR valve, or did you have someone else do it?  It's a pain to reinstall the tube to the underside of the plenum - at least once, I found an EGR-tube gasket installed incorrectly, resulting in a huge vacuum leak, and high idle.<P>Happy hunting  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !  <P>||||reg||1
